#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188214 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188279 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d8dc4e9-47fc-58a2-b650-d9badbcd75d5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Statistics"}] :question/id #uuid "47507b72-86e2-5980-9e59-22d59c9f7279" :question/math-academy-id "e-9918" :question/problem "The weights, in kilograms, of five randomly selected 2-year-old children visiting a pediatrician were measured. The results are shown below.\n\n$12.5, \\quad 11.7,\\quad 13.2,\\quad 12.8, \\quad 12.1$\n\nCalculate the value of the statistic $\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^n X_i^2$ for this particular sample." :question/worked-solution "Suppose $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of $X_i$.\n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nNote the following notation:\n\n- $X_i$ denotes the random variable representing the $i$th observation in a random sample, and\n- $x_i$ is the value of $X_i$ in a specific sample.\n\nFor the given sample, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i}^{2} &= (12.5)^{2} + (11.7)^{2} + (13.2)^{2} + (12.8)^{2} + (12.1)^{2} \\\\\n&= 156.25 + 136.89 + 174.24 + 163.84 + 146.41 \\\\\n&= 777.63\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of our statistic for this particular sample is $777.63\\,\\textrm {kg}^2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188215 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188280 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd3fd748-f238-59b2-badf-54b6e762836c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Statistics"}] :question/id #uuid "11f8e3f1-5110-5db1-83de-99d65ea76de5" :question/math-academy-id "e-9920" :question/problem "A sample $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{8}$ is drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu$ and population standard deviation $\\sigma$. If $\\mu$ and $\\sigma$ are both unknown, which of the following are statistics?\n\n1. $\\displaystyle \\min(X_1, X_2, \\dots, X_8)$\n2. $\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^{4} X_{2i}^2$\n3. $\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^{8} \\left(\\dfrac{X_i}{\\sigma} - 1 \\right)^2$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of $X_i$.\n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each expression.\n\n- Expression I is a statistic. It is a function of the sample $X_1, X_2,\\ldots, X_n$ only.\n- Expression II is a statistic. It is a function of the sample $X_1, X_2,\\ldots, X_n$ only. Note that a statistic doesn't need to include every sample element.\n- Expression III is *not* a statistic. It is a function that contains the population standard deviation $\\sigma$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188550 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188279 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d8dc4e9-47fc-58a2-b650-d9badbcd75d5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Statistics"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8292b5e9-0428-51d2-b904-fd1ece5101d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.11m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185581} {:answer/id #uuid "9f1e9960-530b-54f3-9a15-cf5176444d47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.01m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185582} {:answer/id #uuid "bdc80296-d29a-5269-b527-75225a4ccd2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.24m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185583} {:answer/id #uuid "ffabb546-71fe-56e4-a0ab-5daa5eb63d84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.28m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185584} {:answer/id #uuid "6a320380-ab27-5e2c-a0ae-5ef37e0067d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.34m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185585}] :answer-field/id #uuid "608b6c11-7cbc-5067-8d78-503dd3c27cf3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185580}] :question/id #uuid "4505d831-af23-5580-85dc-c9dcc976f63a" :question/math-academy-id "q-139782" :question/problem "The heights, in meters, of six randomly selected children drawn from a population were measured. The results are shown below.\n\n$1.34, 1.24, 1.42, 1.28, 1.39, 1.44$\n\nCalculate the value of the statistic $∑_(i = 1)^(n)X_{i}$ for this particular sample.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}]}
